# SSH — Mac → Ubuntu (xbox0)

To connect from Mac to the Ubuntu ALL-IN-ONE app server (`192.168.222.200` / xbox0), **always use the `xbox0` host entry / `f0` (or transition `f2`) alias** — not plain `ssh lawsen0@192.168.222.200` (default port and key are rejected).

## Canonical setup

`~/.ssh/config` (Mac, outside git):

```
Host xbox0
  HostName 192.168.222.200
  Port 59221
  User lawsen0
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/xbox2.pem
  IdentitiesOnly yes
  AddKeysToAgent yes
  PreferredAuthentications publickey
```

After `source ~/b`:

```bash
alias f0='ssh xbox0'
alias f2='ssh xbox0'  # transition alias
alias f0tunnel='ssh -L 6969:localhost:6969 xbox0'
alias f2tunnel='ssh -L 6969:localhost:6969 xbox0'
```

| Piece | Value | Why |
|-------|--------|-----|
| VPN | connected first | Host is only reachable over the VPN |
| key | `~/.ssh/xbox2.pem` (mode `600`, owned by the Mac user) | Private key — no `sudo`, no mounted volume |
| `IdentitiesOnly yes` | | Use only that key; ignore ssh-agent / `githubkey` |
| port | `59221` | Non-default SSH port |
| user@host | `lawsen0@192.168.222.200` | ALL-IN-ONE Ubuntu host (xbox0) |

Interactive login: **`f0`** (or `ssh xbox0`; `f2` still aliases to xbox0 during transition). Port forward: `ssh -L <local>:localhost:<remote> xbox0`.

## Agents / scripts (non-interactive)

Do **not** invent a different SSH command. Either use `ssh xbox0 …` or prefer **`scripts/deploy-ssh-mac.sh`** (same port / key / IdentitiesOnly):

```bash
scripts/deploy-ssh-mac.sh lawsen0@192.168.222.200 'hostname'
```

Overrides: `DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` (default `~/.ssh/xbox2.pem`), `DEPLOY_SSH_PORT` (default `59221`), `DEPLOY_SSH_SECRETS`.

## Key passphrase

The key has a passphrase (`Enter passphrase for key '…xbox2.pem':`). The real value lives **only** in the Mac backend env file **`~/.ssh/be/.env`** (outside git):

```bash
SSH_KEY_PASSPHRASE=****
# Optional override for ubuntu-psql.sh; if unset, script falls back to DB_PASSWORD (below)
UBUNTU_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=****
```

When `SSH_KEY_PASSPHRASE` is set there, `scripts/deploy-ssh-mac.sh` answers the prompt non-interactively via `scripts/deploy-ssh-askpass.sh` (`SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force`). **Never** paste the values into this rule, any repo file, or command lines — this repo is pushed to GitHub.

## Postgres admin — `test_user1` (never role `postgres`)

For security, **do not use the Postgres role `postgres` as day-to-day admin** on Ubuntu (or Mac). App + migrations use **`test_user1`**.

Mac and Ubuntu BE env files hold the same shape (values outside git; **never** copy secrets into this rule or the repo):

| Env file | Path |
|----------|------|
| **Mac** | `~/.ssh/be/.env` |
| **Ubuntu** | `/home/lawsen0/.ssh/be/.env` |

```bash
#------ POSTGRES ------
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=6432                 # PgBouncer (app / BE pooler)
DB_NAME=outdatedDBOct2021
DB_USER=test_user1           # DB admin for this project — not role "postgres"
DB_PASSWORD=****             # test_user1 password (same role on Mac + Ubuntu)
DB_SCHEMA=outdateddbsnapshotoct2024
```

| Port | Use |
|------|-----|
| **`6432`** | App / BE via PgBouncer (`DB_PORT` in `.env`) |
| **`50010`** | Direct Postgres Primary — DDL / `scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh` / `isdbsame` |

## Ubuntu Postgres from the Mac (`scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh`)

Ubuntu Postgres listens on `127.0.0.1:50010` (TCP needs a password; `test_user1` peer auth only works for an OS user named `test_user1`). From the Mac, **always** use:

```bash
scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh -c "SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_database_size(current_database()));"
scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh -f be/db/someMigration.sql          # apply a migration on Ubuntu Primary
echo "SELECT count(*) FROM outdateddbsnapshotoct2024.singles;" | scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh
scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh -At -c "SELECT …"                   # extra psql flags pass through
```

- Password (Mac `~/.ssh/be/.env`), first match wins: **`UBUNTU_POSTGRES_PASSWORD`**, else **`DB_PASSWORD`** (same `test_user1` secret as BE). The script sends it as the first line of the SSH stdin and the remote side reads it into `PGPASSWORD`, so it never appears in argv, `ps`, shell history, or Cursor tool output.
- Connects as **`test_user1`** to `outdatedDBOct2021` on `127.0.0.1:50010` via `deploy-ssh-mac.sh`. Overrides: `UBUNTU_PG_HOST`, `UBUNTU_PG_PORT`, `UBUNTU_PG_USER`, `UBUNTU_PG_DB`, `UBUNTU_SSH_HOST`.
- Runs with `ON_ERROR_STOP=1`; exit code is non-zero if any statement fails.
- Writes / DDL go here (single-box Primary). After DDL, run **`isdbsame`** (`scripts/compare-pg-schema.sh`) and report the result (see `all-in-1UbuntuDevelopment.mdc` §2).
- **Never** `cat`/`grep`/`sed` the secrets in `~/.ssh/be/.env` (Mac or Ubuntu) yourself, pass `PGPASSWORD=…` on a command line, or print the password — let the script read it.
- **Never** connect or migrate as role **`postgres`** — use **`test_user1`** only.

## ⚠ `AuthorizedKeysFile` is non-standard — `~/coredump/` is LIVE auth, not junk

xbox0's `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` sets (relative to `$HOME`):

```
AuthorizedKeysFile .ssh/authorized_keys coredump/corrupt_logs_2020.log
```

So `/home/lawsen0/coredump/corrupt_logs_2020.log` **is** an `authorized_keys` file despite the misleading `.log` name, and `~/coredump/` is **never deletable**. Deleting it locks the Mac out of SSH entirely (`Permission denied (publickey)`) while the site, PM2 and Postgres keep running — a silent, outage-free lockout.

- **Never** delete `~/coredump/`, `~/coredump/corrupt_logs_2020.log`, `~/.ssh/`, `.coredump/`, or `~/.sssh/` — and never classify a home folder as "junk" without checking `sshd -T` first.
- Also note `AllowUsers lawsen0`: logins as `root`, `test_user1`, or any other OS user always fail regardless of keys.
- Correct way to confirm before any home-directory cleanup:
  ```bash
  sshd -T | grep -iE 'authorizedkeysfile|allowusers'
  ```

## Rules for agents

- **Never** `ssh lawsen0@192.168.222.200` without `-p 59221`, `IdentitiesOnly=yes`, and `-i ~/.ssh/xbox2.pem` — use `ssh xbox0` or `deploy-ssh-mac.sh`.
- **Never** use agent keys / `githubkey` for this host — IdentitiesOnly + `~/.ssh/xbox2.pem` only.
- `scp` / `rsync` over SSH must use the **same** port, key, and IdentitiesOnly options (e.g. `scp file xbox2:~/`, `rsync -e 'ssh' … xbox2:…`).
- Ubuntu Postgres queries / migrations from the Mac → `scripts/ubuntu-psql.sh` (above); don't hand-roll `ssh … psql` with a password.
- Postgres admin is **`test_user1`** (`DB_USER` / `DB_PASSWORD` in Mac + Ubuntu BE `.env`); **never** use role **`postgres`** for app or DDL.
- On Ubuntu itself, `f0`/`f2` is a no-op reminder (already on the box) — see `myscript/ubuntu/b`.
